<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Wellness and Mindfulness – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Wellness and Mindfulness</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Exercise Helps My Body</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about daily routines. Interactive: Engage in role-plays based on common social situations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe daily wellness routines using appropriate vocabulary (e.g., "I walk to school every morning for exercise." "I stretch before soccer practice." "I take ten deep breaths before studying or taking a test to clear my mind and focus.")<br>Can engage in role-plays about wellness habits (e.g., pretends to explain "I do yoga because it helps me relax.")<br>Can role-play explaining a mindfulness activity (e.g., "Let's breathe deeply to relax our minds and bodies.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to stand up and do a quick "Physical Wellness Check-in." Students mime a simple exercise they do (e.g., stretching, walking, breathing deeply) and say, "I [action] to feel [adjective]." (e.g., "I stretch to feel flexible.", "I breathe deeply to feel calm.") The teacher encourages participation from all students, creating a dynamic start to the speaking lesson.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target vocabulary and sentence structures for sharing wellness practices. For example, the teacher says: "I exercise three times a week to stay fit and focused. It really helps to boost my energy." The teacher also models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "mindfulness" (mind-ful-ness) and "flexibility" (flex-i-bil-i-ty), emphasizing the correct stress. The teacher also demonstrates the minimal pair contrast between /ʊ/ and /uː/ using sentences like: "He could wear a suit to the meeting." and "He cooed at the baby while cleaning the soot from the chimney," asking students to repeat.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using questions related to the warm-up and modeled language.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I practice mindfulness,' what am I doing for my mind? Am I thinking deeply or running fast?" (Expected: thinking deeply, focusing, relaxing)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'exercise helps boost my energy,' what does 'boost' mean? Does it lower my energy or increase it?" (Expected: increase it)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two teenagers discussing their daily wellness routines and how exercise helps their bodies. The dialogue includes target vocabulary (e.g., 'flexibility', 'endurance', 'mental well-being', 'balance', 'hydrate') and grammar (Present simple for general statements, Present continuous for ongoing actions). The genre is an informal conversation, and the register is casual but appropriate for school.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in group discussions on wellness routines, contributing suggestions and solutions to promote mental and physical well-being.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral responses):</b> Students listen to the dialogue and answer gist questions first, then True/False. Teacher pauses the audio if needed.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are the two friends mainly talking about?" "What are some benefits of exercise they mention?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher reads statements, and students verbally respond "True" or "False," justifying their answers briefly. E.g., "One friend says she is currently practicing meditation every morning." (Present continuous) "The other friend believes exercise has many benefits for physical health." (Present simple)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present continuous for ongoing actions, Present simple for general statements, Collocations with health-related verbs (e.g., energy boost, promote health, maintain balance).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> wellness, mindfulness, exercise, energy, benefits, strength, flexibility, health, body, mental well-being, routine, practice, meditation, relaxation, nutrition, balance, endurance, resilience, focus, habit (nouns); boost, improve, strengthen, relax, meditate, exercise, promote, balance, enhance, maintain, walk, stretch, breathe, focus, hydrate (verbs); mental, physical, healthy, strong, flexible, mindful, energized, balanced, calm, peaceful (adjectives).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Stress in multisyllabic and compound words (flexible, flexibility, resilience, resilient, mindfulness), minimal pairs /ʊ/ vs /uː/ (could/cooed, soot/suit).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Sharing wellness practices ("I exercise three times a week to stay fit and focused."), Supporting varied routines ("Some people like to meditate because it helps them manage stress.").</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences on the board using target grammar and vocabulary. In pairs, students orally complete the sentences, ensuring correct word choice and pronunciation. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and pronunciation.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I ____ (exercise/walk) daily to ____ (boost/enhance) my ____ (energy/endurance)." "Right now, I am ____ (practice/practicing) ____ (mindfulness/meditation) to ____ (relax/improve) my ____ (focus/mental well-being)."</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Builder Drill (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> Students work in small groups. The teacher provides a set of sentence frames and word banks (nouns, verbs, adjectives). Students take turns building and saying sentences about wellness routines. The teacher guides them to use both present simple and present continuous.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame: "I [verb] [frequency] to [verb] my [noun]." (e.g., "I stretch every morning to improve my flexibility.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame: "Currently, I am [verb + -ing] because it [verb] my [noun]." (e.g., "Currently, I am hydrating because it promotes my physical health.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus on distinguishing /ʊ/ in 'could' when saying "It *could* help you feel calm" vs. /uː/ in 'suit' if talking about a 'suitable' exercise.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interview & Share (Peer Check):</b> Students interview their partners about their preferred wellness habits and routines. They use the phrases modeled earlier. Afterwards, each student briefly shares one interesting fact about their partner's routine with the class.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What wellness routines do you practice?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I walk to school every morning for exercise. It gives me an energy boost. Sometimes, I also take ten deep breaths to clear my mind."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B also says, "Some people like to meditate because it helps them manage stress."</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students will engage in a role-play titled "My Wellness Story." One student plays a teenager who feels stressed or lacks energy and wants to improve their wellness habits. The other student acts as a "Wellness Buddy" who shares their own successful routines and provides advice, emphasizing how exercise helps their body and mind. Students must use at least 5-7 target vocabulary words, 2-3 examples of Present Simple and Present Continuous, and demonstrate correct pronunciation of multisyllabic words and minimal pairs. They should aim for natural communication and support each other's routines.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions for students:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Work in pairs. Decide who is the "Teenager" and who is the "Wellness Buddy."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teenager: Explain your challenges (e.g., "I feel tired," "I have trouble focusing").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Wellness Buddy: Share your personal wellness routines (how exercise helps your body and mind). Give suggestions and solutions, using phrases like "I do yoga because it helps me relax," or "Let's breathe deeply to relax our minds."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use vocabulary from our lesson (e.g., *mindfulness*, *flexibility*, *energy boost*, *resilience*, *hydrate*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pay attention to your pronunciation, especially multisyllabic words and the sounds in 'could' and 'cooed.'</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each role-play should last about 2-3 minutes.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, and use of target language, taking notes for formative assessment.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe each pair's performance during the role-play.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "My Wellness Story" role-play for the class (or selected pairs, depending on time). Other students provide positive peer feedback focusing on clear communication and good ideas.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially multisyllabic words like "mindfulness," "flexibility," and vowel contrasts /ʊ/ vs /uː/).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (nouns, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Present Simple (for routines/general statements) and Present Continuous (for ongoing actions).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of expression and ability to describe wellness routines and habits.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction and ability to respond appropriately to the partner's input.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today to talk about exercise or wellness was..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel [more/less/the same] confident talking about my daily routines after today's lesson because..."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of describing wellness routines was easiest for me today? What was most challenging?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use the vocabulary and phrases from today's lesson to talk about my own health habits outside of class?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., student engagement in role-plays, successful use of specific grammar points like present continuous for ongoing actions).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for future speaking tasks? (e.g., providing more visual cues for pronunciation, more structured sentence frames for lower-proficiency students, or extended time for specific pair work activities).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are students confident using key vocabulary like 'mindfulness' and 'flexibility' in conversation? Is the distinction between /ʊ/ and /uː/ clear?</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will apply the vocabulary and grammatical structures learned in this speaking lesson to a writing task, composing short paragraphs about their wellness routines and the benefits of exercise for their body, focusing on constructing cohesive sentences.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>